WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.280
VOICE OVER: How well do we
understand facial expressions?

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:05.540
LISA FELDMAN BARRETT: Emotional expressions are

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:11.400
much more nuanced and variable than
people have assumed.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:15.960
VOICE OVER: And do our faces reliably express our feelings?

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:19.020
What emotion is this person feeling?

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:19.889
What about this one?

00:00:19.889 --> 00:00:22.140
RACHAEL JACK: We can use facial
expressions to communicate how we're

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:26.789
feeling and but it can also
reflect different personality traits.

00:00:26.789 --> 00:00:29.780
VOICE OVER: Is this person distraught

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:32.200
or elated?

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:33.780
Furious

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:35.720
or celebratory?

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:43.980
LISA: The interest in facial expressions and
the modern science of emotion really began

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:50.010
in the 1960s with a scientist named
Silvan Tomkins and two of his

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:53.580
postdoctoral fellows Paul Ekman and Cal Izard.

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:54.740
They were very interested in

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:57.960
testing the hypothesis that
everyone around the world

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:03.390
scowls when they're angry, smiles when
they're happy, and correspondingly that

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:09.120
everyone around the world recognises
those facial movements as expressions of emotion.

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:13.640
VOICE OVER: These ideas stood largely
unchallenged for a generation but many

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:16.860
researchers now believe that the
picture is a lot more nuanced.

00:01:16.870 --> 00:01:20.540
LISA: What we're dealing with is highly variable,

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:27.360
highly context sensitive, 
temporarily changing patterns.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:32.040
VOICE OVER: And these contexts are one of the key
factors researchers are investigating as

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:35.260
they try to understand facial
expressions.

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:37.160
LISA: While people are moving their faces

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:41.280
they're also speaking, there are
acoustical changes in the vocalisations

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:45.299
there are body postures. The person
carries around with them a whole

00:01:45.299 --> 00:01:49.409
internal context of their body and then
there's the external context you know

00:01:49.409 --> 00:01:52.079
who else is present? What kind of
situation are they in? What are they doing?

00:01:52.079 --> 00:01:56.040
And then there's a temporal
context and all of that contextual

00:01:56.040 --> 00:02:01.259
information really matters.
So whereas scientists used to focus

00:02:01.259 --> 00:02:06.860
primarily on the face now they're
focusing more on faces in context.

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:11.910
VOICE OVER: Research into expressions has typically
relied on showing subjects posed

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:15.319
photographs of faces
representing different emotions

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:19.940
but photographs like this are often just
faces in isolation with no context at all.

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:25.620
Also, because they are static they
lack the temporal context which could be

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:29.920
read in a moving face.
What's more the researchers have to

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:34.760
start by assigning emotions to
expressions which can introduce biases

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:40.459
and subjectivity. To get around this
researchers are turning to technology to

00:02:40.459 --> 00:02:43.340
find more neutral ways
of studying emotion.

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:44.390
RACHAEL: The approach that we take is

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:48.020
more of a bottom-up, objective approach.

00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:50.090
VOICE OVER: Rachael Jack's research uses a computer

00:02:50.090 --> 00:02:54.080
which randomly generates
dynamic facial expressions.

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:55.250
RACHAEL: We've used this really to

00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:59.840
look at the main differences between
cultures in terms of the six classic

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:05.870
emotions; happy, surprise, fear, disgust,
anger and sad, and we found that East

00:03:05.870 --> 00:03:12.470
Asian facial expressions of these
emotions tend to be signaled primarily

00:03:12.470 --> 00:03:17.209
with the eye region, so the eyebrows and
differences in the eyes. Whereas with

00:03:17.209 --> 00:03:22.280
Westerners the face movements tended to
vary more in the mouth region.

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:28.780
These differences, also, in the location of
expressivity on the face, is mirrored

00:03:28.790 --> 00:03:35.120
very nicely in cultural emoticons.
So in western emoticons what you find is

00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:39.760
that there's two eyes and then the mouth
tends to vary to be able to express

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:44.450
sadness, happiness and so on, and it's the
opposite pattern with East Asian

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:49.220
emoticons in that the mouth is either
minimized or it's completely absent and

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:54.430
it's the variance of the eyes which
communicates different emotion messages.

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:59.380
VOICE OVER: Differences like this are leading some
researchers to believe the expressions

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:05.040
are not as universal as once thought and
that some behaviours might be learnt in childhood.

00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:10.340
LISA: What's happening in early
childhood is emotion knowledge is being

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:14.060
bootstrapped into the brain, into the
brain's wiring. This is also what happens

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:18.590
when you change cultures when you move
from one culture to another culture you

00:04:18.590 --> 00:04:23.840
have to learn what is the nod of a
head or the or the raise of a lip or

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:29.020
the shake of a head you know what do
these things actually mean in this culture.

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:33.560
VOICE OVER: Despite these complexities
the idea that facial expressions could

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:38.500
be used as a way to read emotions has
excited big business.

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:42.380
Leading tech companies have already started
developing algorithms to detect

00:04:42.380 --> 00:04:47.690
expressions and one estimate suggests
the industry could be worth fifty six

00:04:47.690 --> 00:04:54.380
billion dollars by 2024. Systems are
already being trialed and marketed in a

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:55.980
wide variety of fields.

00:04:55.980 --> 00:05:00.110
LISA: For hiring purposes, in educational settings,

00:05:00.110 --> 00:05:05.580
in security settings, and in certain places
even in legal settings.

00:05:05.800 --> 00:05:08.540
VOICE OVER: But despite the
predictions many scientists

00:05:08.540 --> 00:05:12.060
think that claims software could
read emotion may be premature.

00:05:12.060 --> 00:05:15.560
RACHAEL: We often make facial
expressions that convey an emotion

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:21.139
message but doesn't necessarily mean
that we have this emotion inside so

00:05:21.139 --> 00:05:25.580
oftentimes we smile out of politeness, we
want to convey to the other person that

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:30.130
you know they're welcome etc. But this
doesn't necessarily mean that you're

00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:36.530
experiencing an emotion. The other
challenge is that it could be very

00:05:36.530 --> 00:05:42.169
easy to trick automatic facial
expression detection systems. So we may

00:05:42.169 --> 00:05:47.180
want the system to believe that we are
feeling sad or happy and so it'd be

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:54.740
quite easy for a human then to
create, to generate realistic

00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:58.360
facial expressions that could trick a system.

